## 2. The recurring thread

Nothing below has been executed. This is the live technical question the course returns to.

**Step 1 — Recapitalize.** Meg contributes 100% of her CPC stock to a newly formed **Cole Family Holdings LLC**, which issues **1 voting unit and 99 non-voting units**, all to Meg. The LLC's only asset is the CPC stock.

**Step 2 — Seed gift.** Meg makes a **taxable gift of 10 non-voting units** to the **Cole 2026 Irrevocable Trust**, an intentionally defective grantor trust [IDGT — treated as owned by the grantor for income tax, outside the estate for transfer tax]. The attorney proposes drafting it as a **SLAT** [spousal lifetime access trust] with David as a discretionary beneficiary.

**Step 3 — Installment sale.** Meg sells the remaining **89 non-voting units** to the same trust for a **demand promissory note**.

**Step 4 — Retained interest.** Meg keeps the **1 voting unit** in her revocable trust. She controls the LLC after the transaction.

**Step 5 — The annual loop.** CPC pays dividends to the LLC, which distributes 99% to the trust and 1% to Meg. **The trust makes minimum payments — interest only.** The excess accumulates inside the trust. Principal is paid down only when Meg needs cash. Meg, as grantor, pays the income tax on **all** of the trust's income from her own funds.

**Discount claimed.** **30% combined** for lack of control and lack of marketability, on the non-voting units only. The voting unit is valued undiscounted.

