### 3.4 Why it gets worse every year — the case's central checkable defect

Meg's inflow is fixed at $1,358,923 because the note is interest-only. Her tax is not fixed: the trust's accumulating cash earns income that is **also taxed to Meg**.

Assumes trust cash invested at 4.0% and taxed to Meg at 45.75% (37% + 3.8% NIIT + 4.95% Illinois). **[M — an assumption, not a fact; a student who challenges it is right to. The direction does not depend on it.]** Household deficit of $349,350 is $600,000 of living expenses against $450,000 of W-2 comp worth roughly $250,650 after tax.

| Year | Meg receives | Meg owes | Household deficit | Cash she must find | Cumulative | Trust cash |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | $1,358,923 | $1,437,500 | $349,350 | $427,927 | $427,927 | $3,641,077 |
| 3 | $1,358,923 | $1,573,429 | $349,350 | $563,856 | $1,486,341 | $11,365,986 |
| 5 | $1,358,923 | $1,720,449 | $349,350 | $710,876 | $2,833,142 | $19,721,247 |
| 7 | $1,358,923 | $1,879,466 | $349,350 | $869,893 | $4,491,861 | $28,758,298 |
| 9 | $1,358,923 | $2,051,460 | $349,350 | $1,041,887 | **$6,487,952** | **$38,532,773** |

- **Liquid assets — $5,000,000 of brokerage, cash, and T-bills — are exhausted in year 8.**
- By year 9 the trust holds **$38,532,773 of idle cash against a $34,265,000 note it is not repaying.** It could have retired the note in year 8 and did not, because the instruction was "minimum payments."
- Fully liquidating the IRA and 403(b) yields roughly **$2,322,000** after ordinary rates — enough to reach year 11, not further.

### 3.5 The liquidity valve is self-defeating

The note is payable on demand, so Meg can call principal whenever she is short. That is the design. It is also the problem: **every dollar of principal she calls returns to her estate at 100 cents on the dollar**, undoing the discount she paid gift tax to create.

| Line | Amount |
|---|---|
| Cash Meg must find over nine years | $6,487,952 |
| If sourced entirely by calling principal, value returned to the estate | $6,487,952 |
| Federal estate tax on it at 40% | $2,595,181 |
| Illinois estate tax at roughly 16% marginal **[M — verify on Form 700]** | $1,038,072 |
| **Combined transfer-tax cost of the liquidity relief** | **$3,633,253** |

**Every $1 of liquidity relief costs about $0.56 of transfer tax later.**

### 3.6 What the structure buys — the burn, quantified

The pain has a purpose. Every dollar of income tax Meg pays on trust income is a transfer to the trust that consumes **no** gift-tax exemption.

| Line | Amount |
|---|---|
| Value removed at closing (the discount) | $20,185,000 |
| Income tax Meg pays on trust income over nine years — the burn | $15,574,109 |
| **Total value shifted in nine years** | **$35,759,109** |
| Transfer tax avoided on the burn alone, at a 56% combined marginal rate | $8,721,501 |
| Gift-tax exemption consumed | $3,850,000 |

**This is why the structure is worth arguing about.** It shifts $35.8M for $3.85M of exemption. It also cannot be funded past year 8. Both are true, and neither cancels the other.

### 3.7 Why a demand note rather than a term note

| Structure | Rate | Year-1 interest on $34,265,000 |
|---|---|---|
| 9-year term note, mid-term AFR | 4.35% | $1,490,528 |
| Demand note, 2026 blended annual rate | 3.82% | $1,308,923 |
| **Additional value shifted, year 1** | | **$181,604** |

53 basis points cheaper. The cost: Meg can call $34,265,000 at any time against a trust whose only real asset is illiquid units — which is precisely the retained-power fact pattern the Service attacks. **The demand feature is simultaneously the liquidity valve (§3.5) and the §2036 exposure (§10, V3).**

### 3.8 The alternative the attorney's memo also describes, tested and rejected

One passage of the proposal reads as though the trust buys units **incrementally** over time with excess dividends rather than buying 89 units at one closing. Tested on these numbers:

- The trust's 10 seed units entitle it to 10% of $5,000,000 = **$500,000 per year**.
- Units cost $385,000 each, so holdings compound at **12.99% per year**.
- Reaching 99 units takes **19 years**, and every unit after the first is bought at a **higher** price as CPC appreciates — which is the opposite of a freeze.

**Not recommended.** One closing at today's value transfers more, faster, with one appraisal instead of nineteen. This is a genuine ambiguity in the source instruction and is resolved here on arithmetic, not preference. See §10, V14.

